    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Charles I inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon in 1516 and became Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1519, uniting vast lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</li><li>American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tercio">tercios</a> and a council-based government made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s leading power, especially under Philip II.</li><li>War, debt and revolts brought decline after 1640; the childless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II</a>’s death in 1700 passed the throne to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Philip II governed his empire largely on paper, reading and annotating thousands of reports in his own hand from his study, and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">Escorial</a> as palace, monastery and royal tomb.</p></aside>
    </section>

