    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile.jpg" alt="Isabella I of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsabellaofCastile03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was created by a series of dynastic accidents. The marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> in 1469 had joined their crowns in the person of the monarch, while each kingdom kept its own laws and institutions. Their daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-duchess-of-brabant">Joanna</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-handsome">Philip the Handsome</a>, son of Emperor Maximilian I, but Philip died in 1506 and Joanna was judged unfit to rule. When Ferdinand died in January 1516, their eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles of Ghent</a>, already ruler of the Burgundian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, inherited the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, nominally jointly with his mother until her death in 1555, and the Crown of Aragon with its Italian kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. He arrived in Spain in 1517 speaking little Castilian and surrounded by Flemish advisers. In 1519 he was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> as Charles V, adding the Habsburg Austrian lands for a time. At the same moment <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> began the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> (1519–1521), and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> later toppled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> (1532–1533). Within two decades the young king of Spain had become the ruler of the largest collection of territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and of a rapidly expanding empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seville"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seville.jpg" alt="Seville" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sevilla_Cathedral_-_Southeast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ingo Mehling, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, with perhaps six million inhabitants in the late sixteenth century, was the heart of the monarchy and bore most of its taxes. Its economy rested on grain farming, wine and the wool of merino sheep, moved along long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transhumance">transhumance</a> routes under the privileged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesta">Mesta</a> and exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> held the monopoly of trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a>, and convoys of the treasure fleets brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, discovered in 1545, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. The Spanish silver dollar, or piece of eight, circulated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, especially after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a> began sailing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in 1565. The influx of silver contributed to a long rise in prices. Society was dominated by a titled aristocracy and a large lesser nobility of <em>hidalgos</em>, exempt from direct taxes, and by a powerful Church. Religious uniformity was enforced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> and by statutes of ‘purity of blood’ that discriminated against descendants of converted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Muslims. Between 1609 and 1614 Philip III expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a>, descendants of Muslims, around 300,000 people, a heavy blow to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and Aragon. Plague and emigration reduced Castile’s population in the seventeenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia.jpg" alt="Battle of Pavia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Pavia,_1525_(by_Rupert_Heller)_-_Nationalmuseum,_Stockholm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rupert Heller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’s infantry, organised from the 1530s in the famous <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tercio">tercios</a></em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>, was regarded as the best in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for more than a century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>’s armies defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> in 1525, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1527, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1535 and crushed the German Protestant princes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Mühlberg</a> in 1547. Philip II’s forces defeated France at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a> in 1557 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gravelines-1558">Gravelines</a> in 1558, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cateau-cambresis">Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</a> in 1559. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> from Spain and its Italian kingdoms fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">Don John of Austria</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> that broke out in 1566–1568 tied down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-flanders">Army of Flanders</a>, supplied overland by the Spanish Road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, for eighty years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Spanish Armada</a> of 1588, sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, was defeated by the English fleet and storms. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a> Spanish troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> in 1625 and shared in the victory at Nördlingen in 1634, but the French defeated the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi">Battle of Rocroi</a> in 1643, and Portugal won its war of independence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montes-claros">Montes Claros</a> in 1665.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-gomez-de-sandoval-duke-of-lerma"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-gomez-de-sandoval-duke-of-lerma.jpg" alt="Francisco Gómez de Sandoval, Duke of Lerma" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-gomez-de-sandoval-duke-of-lerma">Francisco Gómez de Sandoval, Duke of Lerma</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_ecuestre_del_duque_de_Lerma_(Rubens).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Peter Paul Rubens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The monarchy was a composite state: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, Catalonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and, from 1580, Portugal each kept its own laws, and the king ruled the non-Castilian kingdoms through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> and governors. Government at the centre worked through councils, a system called the polysynodal regime: the Councils of State and War for foreign policy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-castile">Council of Castile</a> for justice and internal government, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-aragon">Councils of Aragon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> for those territories, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a>, founded in 1524, for the Americas, and the Council of Finance. Philip II (r. 1556–1598) fixed the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1561 and governed largely on paper, annotating thousands of reports from his study, while building the palace-monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">El Escorial</a> (1563–1584). His successors delegated power to favourites, or <em>validos</em>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-gomez-de-sandoval-duke-of-lerma">Duke of Lerma</a> under Philip III and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Count-Duke of Olivares</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-arms">Union of Arms</a> of 1626 sought to make the other kingdoms share Castile’s military burden. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-castile">Cortes of Castile</a> voted taxes such as the <em>millones</em>, but Aragon and Catalonia defended their privileges, or <em>fueros</em>. Repeated suspensions of payment to creditors, from 1557 onwards, revealed the strain on royal finances.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/las-meninas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/las-meninas.jpg" alt="Las Meninas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/las-meninas">Las Meninas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Las_Meninas,_by_Diego_Vel%C3%A1zquez,_from_Prado_in_Google_Earth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Velázquez, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was the first global empire on which, in a phrase of the time, the sun never set. It spread the Castilian language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and Spanish institutions across most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> drove trade from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It was the chief power of the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignatius-of-loyola">Ignatius of Loyola</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Society of Jesus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-of-avila">Teresa of Ávila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-the-cross">John of the Cross</a> reformed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelites</a>, and Spanish theologians shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-vitoria">Francisco de Vitoria</a> and his successors developed ideas of natural law, the rights of peoples and economics that influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, and the debate at Valladolid in 1550–1551 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-gines-de-sepulveda">Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</a> examined the justice of conquest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-golden-age">Spanish Golden Age</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote">Don Quixote</a></em> (1605–1615), the plays of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega">Lope de Vega</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-calderon-de-la-barca">Pedro Calderón de la Barca</a>, the poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-gongora">Luis de Góngora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-quevedo">Francisco de Quevedo</a>, and the paintings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-greco">El Greco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez">Diego Velázquez</a>, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/las-meninas">Las Meninas</a></em> dates from 1656, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-zurbaran">Francisco de Zurbarán</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-esteban-murillo">Bartolomé Esteban Murillo</a>. Spanish dress, court ceremonial and military methods were imitated across Europe.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi.jpg" alt="Battle of Rocroi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi">Battle of Rocroi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Bataille_de_Rocroi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sauveur Lecomte, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The cost of fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> at once exhausted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. In 1640 Catalonia and Portugal revolted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> rose in 1647. The defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rocroi">Rocroi</a> in 1643 ended the reputation of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tercio">tercios</a></em>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-munster">Peace of Münster</a> in 1648 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Peace of the Pyrenees</a> in 1659 ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and Artois to France, and in 1668 Spain accepted Portuguese independence. Under the sickly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II</a> (r. 1665–1700) France took Franche-Comté in 1678 and further towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, though historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-kamen">Henry Kamen</a> have argued that parts of Spain’s economy began to recover in the later seventeenth century. Charles II had no children, and European powers negotiated partition treaties for his inheritance. In his will of October 1700 he left the whole monarchy to Philip, Duke of Anjou, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV of France</a>, and died on 1 November 1700. Philip V’s accession ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule in Spain and provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> (1701–1714), after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> kept Spain and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> while the Netherlands and the Italian possessions passed mostly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Aragon, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, Portugal and a vast American empire under one dynasty, dominating sixteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tercio">tercios</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> and producing the Golden Age of Cervantes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez">Velázquez</a>, before war and debt brought decline.</em></p>

